Add an EventWriteTransfer redirector, so a Chromium base built with a current
Windows SDK still loads on Windows XP.

base/trace_event/trace_event_etw_export_win.cc already carries an XP shim for
the manifest-based ETW provider API: it defines functions called EventRegister,
EventWrite and EventUnregister, resolves the real ones out of advapi32 with
GetProcAddress, and returns 0 when they are absent. Its own comment says so --
"Try to find the ETW functions. This will fail on XP." M49 was the last
Chromium to support XP and this is how it did it.

The calls being intercepted come from chrome_events_win.h, which mc.exe
generates from chrome_events_win.man at build time. That is where this breaks:
the mc.exe of 2016 generated calls to EventWrite, and the one in the current
Windows SDK generates EventWriteTransfer instead. Nothing intercepts that, so
it is left as a static advapi32 import -- and a statically imported symbol the
OS does not export is fatal at load, before any process code runs. The result
is that Sawbuck.exe dies on XP with

  The procedure entry point EventWriteTransfer could not be located in the
  dynamic link library ADVAPI32.dll

even though every other piece of XP support is in place. Nothing in Chromium's
source mentions EventWriteTransfer; the import is entirely a product of the
newer message compiler.

So this adds the fourth redirector, in the shape of the three already there: a
typedef, a resolved pointer, the GetProcAddress beside its siblings, and a
function that forwards when the real one exists and returns 0 when it does not.
Returning 0 drops the event, which is the only outcome available on a system
with no manifest-based ETW to write into, and matches what the other three do.

Note on winwrap. winwrap also supplies EventWriteTransfer, and does so on
different terms -- it returns ERROR_INVALID_HANDLE, because on XP the REGHANDLE
genuinely is invalid and winwrap's rule is that a function which cannot do its
job fails rather than returning a plausible value. Both are correct for where
they live: this patch follows the convention of the file it is in. They do not
conflict either, since base's call sites bind to the local definition -- which
is demonstrable, as EventRegister, EventWrite and EventUnregister appear
nowhere in the finished binary's import table while EventWriteTransfer did.
This patch is what makes a build without winwrap load; winwrap is what covers
any other consumer of the same API.

--- a/base/trace_event/trace_event_etw_export_win.cc
+++ b/base/trace_event/trace_event_etw_export_win.cc
@@ -40,10 +40,21 @@
                                       ULONG UserDataCount,
                                       PEVENT_DATA_DESCRIPTOR UserData);
 typedef ULONG(__stdcall* tEventUnregister)(REGHANDLE RegHandle);
+// Not in the original. The current Windows SDK's mc.exe generates calls to
+// EventWriteTransfer where the one this file was written against generated
+// EventWrite, so the three redirectors below no longer cover everything
+// chrome_events_win.h emits.
+typedef ULONG(__stdcall* tEventWriteTransfer)(REGHANDLE RegHandle,
+                                              PCEVENT_DESCRIPTOR EventDescriptor,
+                                              LPCGUID ActivityId,
+                                              LPCGUID RelatedActivityId,
+                                              ULONG UserDataCount,
+                                              PEVENT_DATA_DESCRIPTOR UserData);
 
 tEventRegister EventRegisterProc = nullptr;
 tEventWrite EventWriteProc = nullptr;
 tEventUnregister EventUnregisterProc = nullptr;
+tEventWriteTransfer EventWriteTransferProc = nullptr;
 
 // |kFilteredEventGroupNames| contains the event categories that can be
 // exported individually. These categories can be enabled by passing the correct
@@ -130,6 +141,26 @@
   return 0;
 }
 
+// Redirector function for EventWriteTransfer. Called by macros in
+// chrome_events_win.h.
+//
+// Without this the call falls through to a static advapi32 import, and the
+// process fails to load on XP before any of its own code runs -- which is the
+// one thing the three redirectors above exist to prevent. Returns 0 when the
+// function is absent, matching its siblings: the event is dropped, which is
+// all that can happen on a system with no manifest-based ETW to write into.
+ULONG EVNTAPI EventWriteTransfer(REGHANDLE RegHandle,
+                                 PCEVENT_DESCRIPTOR EventDescriptor,
+                                 LPCGUID ActivityId,
+                                 LPCGUID RelatedActivityId,
+                                 ULONG UserDataCount,
+                                 PEVENT_DATA_DESCRIPTOR UserData) {
+  if (EventWriteTransferProc)
+    return EventWriteTransferProc(RegHandle, EventDescriptor, ActivityId,
+                                  RelatedActivityId, UserDataCount, UserData);
+  return 0;
+}
+
 namespace base {
 namespace trace_event {
 
@@ -169,6 +200,8 @@
         ::GetProcAddress(AdvapiDLL, "EventWrite"));
     EventUnregisterProc = reinterpret_cast<tEventUnregister>(
         ::GetProcAddress(AdvapiDLL, "EventUnregister"));
+    EventWriteTransferProc = reinterpret_cast<tEventWriteTransfer>(
+        ::GetProcAddress(AdvapiDLL, "EventWriteTransfer"));
 
     // Register the ETW provider. If registration fails then the event logging
     // calls will fail (on XP this call will do nothing).
